WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.856
From a dataset to a gradient.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:05.699
In week 2 you built a dataset of example conversations.

3
00:00:05.979 --> 00:00:21.061
Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) is the procedure that turns those examples into weight updates, and it is less exotic than it sounds: it is the same next-token prediction objective the model was pretrained with, applied to your data,

4
00:00:21.341 --> 00:00:25.245
usually with the loss restricted to the part you want the model to produce.

5
00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:28.320
This is also, in miniature, how assistants are made.

6
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:43.960
In the InstructGPT paper (Ouyang and colleagues, 2022), the first stage of turning a pretrained model into an assistant was supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations written by labelers, before any reinforcement learning.

7
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:54.693
Your runs this week are the same kind of step on a much smaller scale: a few hundred or thousand examples that teach one behavior, such as returning a fixed JSON format.

8
00:00:54.973 --> 00:01:09.373
This lesson builds the objective precisely, because almost every surprise in a fine-tuning run (a loss that won’t move, a loss that hits zero, a model that won’t stop generating) traces back to which tokens are scored and how.

9
00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:20.939
Lesson 8 then changes which parameters are trained (LoRA), and lesson 9 runs the whole thing with Hugging Face’s PEFT and TRL libraries, or MLX-LM on a Mac.
